## 2. Sympathetic String Under Fogged Glass

**Story & Context:** Solveig notices that one sympathetic string becomes unusually prominent when she faces the fogged glass wall rather than the center of the room. Davi treats the difference as a spatial arrangement rule. The track alternates between close, dry bowed phrases and sections where only the sympathetic-string bus remains while the direct fiddle signal disappears.

Mara uses three prepared dulcitone tones, each damped to a different decay length. Elias plays baritone-sax notes that begin in breath and only gradually acquire pitch. The tarraxo groove stays almost unchanged for the entire track: one low kick pattern, one clipped percussion answer, and restrained sub movement.

At the peak, the direct fiddle vanishes for eight bars. The listener hears only residual strings, breath, floor contact, and the same slow groove, making absence rather than addition the dramatic event.

